Give the public API default visibility, so -fvisibility=hidden leaves an ABI.

zlib declares its entire public API through two macros that expand to nothing
on ELF: ZEXTERN is `extern` and ZEXPORT is empty. That is correct only while
the build has default visibility. This tree compiles everything with
-fvisibility=hidden, which is CFI's precondition rather than a preference, and
under it every one of zlib's 88 exported symbols becomes STV_HIDDEN.

The result is not a compile error. It is a 13 KB libz.so that exports nothing
at all -- `nm --dynamic --defined-only` returns zero lines -- and the first
thing to notice is whatever links against it next. Here that was zlib's own
test programs, in the same cmake run:

    ld.lld: error: undefined symbol: gzopen
    >>> referenced by minigzip.c:551 (/build/src/zlib/test/minigzip.c:551)
    ld.lld: error: undefined symbol: deflate
    ld.lld: error: undefined symbol: inflate

Had the recipe not built them, the empty library would have installed cleanly
and the failure would have surfaced four layers later in systemd.

A version script does not help and is not an alternative: the linker turns a
hidden symbol into a local one before a version node can claim it, so
zlib.map's global list and --export-dynamic-symbol both come out empty too.
Measured, not assumed.

ZEXPORT rather than ZEXTERN because ZEXPORT is the macro that sits between the
return type and the name in every declaration -- `ZEXTERN int ZEXPORT
deflate(...)` -- which is exactly where GNU C accepts an attribute, and because
the header guards it with #ifndef, which is upstream saying it is the intended
hook. Patched in zconf.h.cmakein rather than zconf.h: the cmake build
configures that file into the build directory and renames the shipped zconf.h
out of the way, so a patch to the shipped one would apply cleanly and do
nothing. It is also the copy that gets installed, so a consumer compiled with
-fvisibility=hidden declares zlib's functions with default visibility too.

Verified against the tree's exact flag set: the patched library exports the
same 88 symbols, with the same version nodes, as an unpatched zlib built with
no hardening at all and the same linker; the test programs link; and both
example binaries run clean.

Not upstreamable as-is. Upstream supports builds that do not set
-fvisibility=hidden, and the ELF test here is coarser than zlib's usual
feature detection.

--- a/zconf.h.cmakein
+++ b/zconf.h.cmakein
@@ -381,10 +381,18 @@
 #  define ZEXTERN extern
 #endif
 #ifndef ZEXPORT
-#  define ZEXPORT
+#  if defined(__ELF__) && (defined(__GNUC__) || defined(__clang__))
+#    define ZEXPORT __attribute__((visibility("default")))
+#  else
+#    define ZEXPORT
+#  endif
 #endif
 #ifndef ZEXPORTVA
-#  define ZEXPORTVA
+#  if defined(__ELF__) && (defined(__GNUC__) || defined(__clang__))
+#    define ZEXPORTVA __attribute__((visibility("default")))
+#  else
+#    define ZEXPORTVA
+#  endif
 #endif
 
 #ifndef FAR
